  "judge:reproduce#0": {
    "verdict": "REJECT",
    "severity": "FYI",
    "evidence": "The demonstration stops before a contradiction with a stated requirement. Root \u00a71 and task super-plan-ufz.2 explicitly require retaining resources needed by surviving or unverified owned processes and reporting that uncertainty. Root \u00a72 and task super-plan-ufz.12 require reconciliation before affected-work admission, reuse of valid landings, and preservation of unexplained discrepancies; they do not instruct resume to reuse a surviving process's mutable workspace or accept competing results. A failure story therefore needs an additional implementation choice\u2014overwriting retained resources or admitting conflicting attempts without isolation\u2014which is neither required nor permitted by the preservation contract. Waiting, reconnecting, verifying termination, or isolating a replacement are compatible implementations of the approved minimal mechanism. The artifact does not promise exactly-once attempts or prescribe one survivor-admission protocol, and the finding does not establish that choosing among these implementations is an unanswered load-bearing design decision."
  },
  "judge:refute#0": {
    "verdict": "REJECT",
    "severity": "FYI",
    "evidence": "(a) The artifact already requires retention and identification of resources needed by surviving or unverified owned processes (\u00a71; task super-plan-ufz.2), durable holds and obligations (.4), and reconciliation before resumed admission, with persisted holds constraining affected work (.12). It does not select a specific survivor-handling strategy. (b) \u00a71 explicitly allows code delivery and incomplete cleanup to coexist as separate reported outcomes; process uncertainty is not specified as an automatic execution hold. (c) The finding adds a stronger attempt-exclusivity contract: waiting, reconnecting, or fencing a replacement against the previous attempt. The approved design promises recoverability and resource preservation, without requiring exactly-once execution or prescribing those implementation choices. (d) The scenario establishes unfinished work and retained resources, but does not establish that resume must reuse the survivor's workspace, overwrite its evidence, or accept conflicting results. Those additional premises are needed to demonstrate lost work or another violation. Under roast-context.md's instruction that compatible low-level choices alone are not design gaps, the absence of a selected retry strategy does not establish a material contradiction or lost intended effect."
  },
  "judge:ground#0": {
    "verdict": "REJECT",
    "severity": "FYI",
    "evidence": "The quoted cleanup and recovery provisions are accurate, and the artifact does not prescribe a specific survivor-to-admission policy. However, the finding's load-bearing inference\u2014that an unfinished survivor must contend with a replacement attempt or have its eventual result accepted\u2014is not established by the design. Neither \u00a72 nor task super-plan-ufz.12 requires immediate retry or reuse of a retained workspace. Existing holds explicitly constrain resumed admission; \u00a71 requires preserving needed resources and propagating uncertainty; task .14 checks preservation across interruption/retry and recovery/admission boundaries. Waiting, holding affected work, or isolating a replacement remain compatible implementation choices. Without a contradictory admission requirement or a demonstrated unavoidable loss of a stated guarantee, choosing among those mechanisms is implementation detail under the expressly approved minimal-mechanism scope."
  },
  "judge:reproduce#1": {
    "verdict": "REJECT",
    "severity": "FYI",
    "evidence": "The failure story stops before contradicting a stated requirement. Section 1 and task super-plan-ufz.1 require reviews to remain pinned while integration advances; section 3 and task .5 retain source identity so evidence remains attributable. Section 3 and task .6 require satisfaction of required reviews, measurements and human gates, while preserving existing acceptance ownership. Neither defines every integration advance as creating a new required review or promises that acceptance requires a review of the exact final tree. Task .14 explicitly requires integrated-tree regression evidence, without claiming that measurements replace review. Thus a review of A followed by measurements of B establishes two separately attributed facts, which is permitted; the finding needs an additional premise that the review of A is inapplicable to a specific required review obligation for B. The artifact does not establish that premise. A general review-invalidation policy could be useful, but its absence alone does not demonstrate lost promised behavior under this bounded design."
  },
  "judge:refute#1": {
    "verdict": "REJECT",
    "severity": "FYI",
    "evidence": "(a) The artifact already separates source-bound evidence from acceptance: \u00a73 requires evidence identifying its source tree and independently supported implementation, review, measurement and acceptance facts; task .5 carries that identity, and .6 retains required reviews and existing super-auto final-sweep ownership. (b) \u00a71 and task .1 explicitly permit integration to advance while review remains pinned. That makes a review evidence about its captured scope; it does not claim that review covers subsequent changes. (c) The finding introduces an additional general review-invalidation policy that the bounded design does not promise. Task .6 preserves existing acceptance ownership, and compatible implementation choices need not be fully specified. (d) The proposed A-review/B-measurement sequence does not itself demonstrate a violated requirement: independently reviewed tasks can contribute to B while measurements exercise their integration. No cited provision allows an unmet required review to be replaced by measurements or declares later unreviewed changes covered by A. A material finding would need to identify a required review whose satisfaction the artifact actually misattributes; the absence of a universal invalidation rule alone does not establish that lost effect."
  },
  "judge:ground#1": {
    "verdict": "REJECT",
    "severity": "FYI",
    "evidence": "Read the complete parent and nested specs and all settled task descriptions/acceptance, and searched for review applicability, invalidation, supersession, source identity and integration acceptance mechanisms. The artifact does not prescribe a new review-invalidation policy, but that omission does not establish the claimed material gap. Section 1 and task .1 guarantee stable review inputs while concurrent integration proceeds; they do not declare that the captured review accepts subsequent changes. Section 3 and task .6 preserve independently supported required reviews and existing caller acceptance ownership. Task .14 additionally checks integrated-tree acceptance and permits focused repairs. The finding assumes an additional requirement that every later accepted tree must have explicitly adjudicated coverage from the earlier pinned review, without identifying a stated requirement or existing acceptance obligation that these changes remove. A passing review of A plus measurements of B does not, under the stated contract, establish that required reviews for B are satisfied. Review invalidation details remain a compatible implementation choice rather than a demonstrated contradiction or lost promised effect."
  },
  "judge:reproduce#2": {
    "verdict": "REJECT",
    "severity": "FYI",
    "evidence": "The demonstration breaks at the claim that linking a new occurrence to F allows F's historical resolution to dispose of that occurrence. The nested spec preserves immutable invocation/artifact/section/occurrence identity, requires supporting verification of the relevant fix, and explicitly assigns semantic support judgments to the agent. Its Goal requires preserving actionable findings until a supported disposition accounts for them; parent \u00a74 imposes the same invariant. Task super-plan-ufz.8.4 consumes complete occurrence extraction and requires supported dispositions with explicit references and basis. Thus a supported historical resolution remains valid historical evidence but does not establish that newly reported actionable evidence is resolved. Concealing that evidence would violate the existing contract. The artifact leaves the representation and projection algorithm open, but the finding does not demonstrate a contradictory constraint, unavailable required information, or a load-bearing undecided policy. A recurrence acceptance example could strengthen coverage; its absence alone does not establish the claimed material gap."
  },
  "judge:refute#2": {
    "verdict": "REJECT",
    "severity": "FYI",
    "evidence": "(a) The nested spec's Identity and supported disposition section already assigns semantic applicability to agent judgment: 'the agent judges whether the evidence supports the semantic disposition.' Occurrences retain immutable invocation/artifact/section/position identities; explicit linking preserves those occurrences rather than replacing them. Parent \u00a73 additionally binds evidence to the tested source tree. An earlier verified fix therefore does not establish support for dismissing a later occurrence reporting recurrence. (b) Concealing actionable recurrence is not an accepted tradeoff: parent \u00a74 requires actionable findings to remain accounted for until supported disposition, and the nested goal distinguishes current verdicts from outstanding findings. (c) Recurrence is within scope, but the finding demands a separately specified temporal projection policy where the approved design permits compatible implementation choices under its existing semantic-support rule. (d) Hiding recurrence would materially violate the contract; however, the finding establishes neither a contradictory requirement nor a required behavior that produces that outcome. An additional recurrence acceptance example could strengthen coverage, but its absence alone does not establish a material design gap."
  },
  "judge:ground#2": {
    "verdict": "REJECT",
    "severity": "FYI",
    "evidence": "The artifact leaves the representation of recurrence unspecified, but already assigns disposition applicability to semantic judgment: the nested design requires immutable occurrence identities, resolution naming the finding with verification of the relevant fix, and an agent judging whether evidence supports the disposition. Task super-plan-ufz.8.4 repeats that division between mechanical projection and semantic support. Explicitly linking appearances establishes identity; it does not authorize automatically inheriting a historical resolution despite new actionable evidence. Searches across both specs and task descriptions found no dedicated reopen example, but choosing occurrence-scoped coverage or an explicit updated disposition is a compatible implementation detail under the approved minimal-mechanism approach. The finding does not establish a contradictory constraint or unavoidable loss of the promised continuity."
  },
  "judge:reproduce#3": {
    "verdict": "CONFIRM",
    "severity": "Should-fix",
    "evidence": "The contradiction is present in settled-tree.json, task super-plan-ufz.8.4 Acceptance: \u201cKey-only punch-list output contains outstanding deferred findings, including verified fixes supplied elsewhere.\u201d Concrete case: (1) retain deferred finding F; (2) another task supplies its fix; (3) explicitly resolve F with supporting verification, satisfying the nested spec\u2019s Identity and supported disposition contract. The root spec \u00a74 then requires F to leave the punch list: \u201cA deferred item fixed elsewhere leaves the punch list after verification.\u201d The nested spec Acceptance likewise requires removal after supported resolution, including fixes supplied elsewhere; parent task super-plan-ufz.8 agrees. However, implementing the leaf\u2019s explicit inclusion criterion retains F because it is a verified fix supplied elsewhere. Conversely, removing F satisfies the governing specs but fails that leaf criterion literally. The surrounding \u201coutstanding\u201d qualification and supported-disposition criterion suggest the intended behavior, but they do not reconcile the opposite membership example. This is a material acceptance contradiction for the task implementing the projection and its fixtures; correcting that single criterion avoids implementing or testing retention of discharged obligations."
  },
  "judge:refute#3": {
    "verdict": "CONFIRM",
    "severity": "Should-fix",
    "evidence": "(a) Equivalent mechanisms exist, but contradict the leaf criterion rather than reconcile it: root \u00a74 says a deferred item fixed elsewhere 'leaves the punch list after verification'; nested Acceptance requires removal after explicit supported resolution/rejection, including fixes supplied elsewhere; parent task .8 repeats that removal rule. Yet .8.4 Acceptance says the output 'contains outstanding deferred findings, including verified fixes supplied elsewhere.' Its inclusion clause prescribes the opposite membership for a supported, verified external fix. (b) No documented tradeoff permits keeping resolved findings outstanding; the leaf itself recognizes supported resolution. (c) Correct punch-list membership is explicitly within scope, requiring no additional mechanism. (d) This affects the projected remaining obligations consumed by .8.3 resume, Remaining and status, so the conflicting executable acceptance target risks incorrect reporting and avoidable rework."
  },
  "judge:ground#3": {
    "verdict": "CONFIRM",
    "severity": "Should-fix",
    "evidence": "The internal premises hold. settled-tree.json task super-plan-ufz.8.4 explicitly requires: \u201cKey-only punch-list output contains outstanding deferred findings, including verified fixes supplied elsewhere.\u201d Root spec \u00a74 instead states: \u201cA deferred item fixed elsewhere leaves the punch list after verification.\u201d The nested finding-continuity spec requires supported resolution for externally supplied fixes and says deferred findings leave the punch list after explicit supported resolution or rejection. Parent task super-plan-ufz.8 likewise requires externally fixed items to leave after supported verification. Thus .8.4's inclusion clause gives the projection implementer a contradictory acceptance target for an externally fixed finding with supported resolution. This affects the punch-list output consumed by resume, Remaining and status through .8.3; correcting that clause is needed to preserve the stated outstanding-only contract."
  },
  "reporter": {
    "verdict": "Should-fix (1 confirmed)",
    "reportMarkdown": "super-roast verdict: Should-fix (1 confirmed)\nmode: design        iteration: 1 of 3\nprofile (assumed): Internal local developer tooling. Failures risk developer work and review evidence; no production service or privileged third-party surface is introduced. Stated-purpose and real-data loss severity floors apply.\ninputs: docs/superpowers/specs/2026-10-16-workflow-reliability-design.md + nested finding-continuity spec + settled-tree.json @ e0109aa8\ncoverage: scouts 8/8 (premortem, completeness, yagni, failure-mode, feasibility, domain:workflow-orchestration, domain:distributed-systems, domain:data-pipeline) \u00b7 raw 6 \u2192 deduped 4 \u2192 panel 4 \u00b7 spot 0 \u00b7 promoted 0 \u00b7 judge completion 100% \u00b7 remainder-capped: 0\nindependence: same-family (OpenAI GPT) \u2014 seat-differentiated panel \u00b7 rung: manual fan-out\nseat-agreement: panels 4 \u00b7 rr 1.00 \u00b7 rg 1.00 \u00b7 fg 1.00 \u00b7 unanimous 1.00 \u00b7 ground-loo 1.00 (n=4) \u00b7 reproduce 1/3/0 \u00b7 refute 1/3/0 \u00b7 ground 1/3/0\n\n## Confirmed findings\n- [Should-fix] docs/superpowers/runs/2026-10-16-workflow-reliability/settled-tree.json, super-plan-ufz.8.4 Acceptance; docs/superpowers/specs/2026-10-16-workflow-reliability-design.md:57\u201361; nested finding-continuity spec:38; docs/superpowers/runs/2026-10-16-workflow-reliability/settled-tree.json, super-plan-ufz.8.4 Acceptance; root spec section 4; nested finding-continuity spec Acceptance; settled-tree.json, super-plan-ufz.8.4 Acceptance; root spec \u00a74; nested finding-continuity spec Acceptance \u2014 Task .8.4 requires inclusion of verified fixes supplied elsewhere, contradicting the governing specifications\u2019 removal rule after supported resolution.\n  verdict: confirmed (reproduce \u2713 / refute \u2717-survived / ground \u2713)\n  evidence: All three seats confirm that .8.4 requires output that \u201ccontains outstanding deferred findings, including verified fixes supplied elsewhere.\u201d Root \u00a74 instead says an item fixed elsewhere \u201cleaves the punch list after verification.\u201d The nested specification and parent task .8 require removal after supported resolution. The refute seat confirms that these criteria prescribe opposite membership for the same supported external fix. The conflicting criterion affects the projection consumed by resume, Remaining and status.\n  fix-shape hint: Make .8.4 explicitly require exclusion after supported resolution, including verified fixes supplied elsewhere, and align its acceptance fixture.\n\n## Not verified (beyond panel cap)\n- none\n\n## Not verified (dedupe failed or judge lost)\n- none\n\n## Beyond remainder cap (count only)\n- none\n\n## Rejected (with reason)\n- Root spec \u00a71 Workspace and process safety and \u00a72 Scheduling and recovery; settled-tree.json task super-plan-ufz.12 \u2014 Resumed admission lacks a survivor-handling rule.\n  reason: All three seats reject the claimed gap. Resource preservation, uncertainty reporting and reconciliation already constrain recovery. The failure story additionally assumes conflicting workspace reuse or competing result acceptance, neither required by the artifact. Waiting, holding affected work or isolating a replacement remain compatible implementations under the approved minimal-mechanism scope.\n- docs/superpowers/specs/2026-10-16-workflow-reliability-design.md:19\u201325,43\u201347; settled-tree.json tasks super-plan-ufz.1, super-plan-ufz.6 and super-plan-ufz.14 \u2014 Integration can advance beyond a pinned review without a general review-invalidation rule.\n  reason: All three seats reject the claimed gap. Source-bound reviews remain attributable, required reviews retain their acceptance ownership, and integrated-tree measurements are separate evidence. The finding identifies no required review that the artifact actually misattributes or permits measurements to replace. A universal invalidation policy is an additional requirement.\n- docs/superpowers/runs/2026-10-16-workflow-reliability/2026-10-16-finding-continuity-design.md:16\u201324; settled-tree.json task super-plan-ufz.8.4 \u2014 A later linked actionable occurrence may inherit an earlier resolution.\n  reason: All three seats reject the claimed gap. Immutable occurrence identities and semantic support judgments already require a supported disposition to account for actionable evidence. A historical fix does not establish support for resolving a later recurrence. Automatic inheritance despite new actionable evidence would violate the existing contract; the finding establishes no requirement that produces that behavior.\n\n## Unverified nits (spot-checked)\n- none\n\n## Escalations (need human)\n- none",
    "confirmedCount": 1,
    "escalations": []
  }
}

